seg-f5356cbf3675 = What counts as a time series

# text
seg-ba43cf36ac20 = The result documents differ kind by kind, so the exporter recognises the shapes the engine emits rather than a list of kinds:

# text
# c1: t
# c2: t_s
# c3: t_hours
# c4: t_min
# c5: t_days
# c6: k
# c7: epochs
# c8: quantum.series[].error_ns
# c9: classical.series[].error_ns
seg-952d09156712 = <b>An array of records with a time field</b> (<c1/>, <c2/>, <c3/>, <c4/>, <c5/>, or <c6/> on an <c7/> array) that strictly increases along the array. Every other numeric field becomes a trace; the same field under two roots (<c8/> and <c9/>) shares a panel.

# text
# c1: t_s
# c2: t
# c3: times_s
# c4: channels
# c5: {unit, values}
# c6: campaign
# c7: spectrum
# c8: *_hz
# c9: spectrum
seg-2c3abce3f70a = <b>An object with a strictly increasing time array</b> (<c1/>, <c2/>, <c3/>, …) beside arrays of the same length: sibling numeric arrays, a <c4/> map of <c5/> records (the <c6/> timeline), an array of records holding such arrays (the <c7/> bands, a constellation's satellite tracks), and a two-dimensional array whose rows match the time axis with a sibling <c8/> frequency axis (the <c9/> waterfall).

# text
# c1: phases
# c2: name
# c3: t0_s
# c4: t1_s
# c5: events
# c6: t_s
# c7: label
# c8: alarm
# c9: *_t_s
seg-0edf8c38f9eb = Beside that time array, <c1/> (<c2/>, <c3/>, <c4/>) become the phase strip and <c5/> (<c6/>, <c7/>, <c8/>) become the markers. A band's <c9/> scalars (its first loss of lock, its lowest carrier-to-noise density ratio) are marked too.

# text
# c1: omitted
seg-608c4fed9e98 = Traces with a unit share a panel when their magnitudes are within a factor of { "100" }, so a { "50" } ns guard does not flatten a { "2" } ns error. At most { "6" } panels of { "6" } traces are drawn; the rest are listed under <c1/>. Traces over { "2,400" } samples are thinned by an even stride that keeps the last sample, and a waterfall over { "96" } × { "120" } cells is averaged down (in power, for decibel cells, so a narrow jammer is not diluted).

# text
# c1: no time series to animate
# c2: cargo test --test animation -- --nocapture
seg-5366f477e688 = A kind whose result carries none of these shapes (a link budget, a single-epoch geometry, a statistics-only Monte Carlo summary, or the telecom holdover, whose result publishes the record's hash but not the record) is refused with <c1/>, and nothing is written. With kshana { $version }, { "94" } of the { "192" } runnable scenario files (every file except a study suite) animate and { "98" } are refused. <c2/> prints the census.

# text
# c1: leo-pass
# c2: leo-pnt-chain
# c3: leo-ppp
# c4: leo-pvt
# c5: trace = true
# c6: leo-navmsg
# c7: leo-signal
# c8: ntn-positioning
seg-c386389e4f1d = Among the LEO kinds, every bundled <c1/>, <c2/> and <c3/> scenario animates. <c4/> animates in its Doppler, joint-positioning and timing modes (a timing run needs <c5/>, which makes every row report its time error and predicted sigma at every epoch) and is refused in its polar-coverage mode. <c6/> animates its mid-pass update and is refused for the encoding, fit-interval and model-comparison analyses. <c7/> and <c8/> results carry no time axis and are refused.

